## Tauri 2.0 & Nextjs 15 | CRUD | Sqlite | Drizzle

This is a desktop application which also must run on web. It's built with Nextjs 15+, Tauri 2.0. Drizzle-orm with drizzle-kit, sqlite via Turso and styling via TailwindCSS.

Use Tauri 2.0 API only.

## Core knowledge
- use bun
- Write modular code
- Write as performant and seperated as possible.
- Use newest hooks like `useOptimistic` etc.
- NEVER write classes, also NEVER write arrow constants. Only use pure functtional styntax e.g. `function Foo({bar})`
- NEVER write interfaces, use `type` instead. Only use interfaces if stricly necessary.
- Prefix EVERY type with `T` e.g. `TFoo`. If there is only one type which isn't exported we call the type `TProps` by default.
- A page may never contain logic, only a `view` which sits over at `src/views`.

## Base Types & Schemas
- Every entity must extend `TBaseEntity` from `@/shared/types/base`
- Base entity includes: `id`, `createdAt`, `updatedAt`
- Use schema helpers from `@/api/db/schema/base-entity`:
  - `createTable()` for creating tables with base fields
  - `TBaseTable<T>` for inferring entity types from tables
  - `TBaseInsert<T>` for inferring insert types
- Schema example:
```typescript
import { text } from "drizzle-orm/sqlite-core";
import { createTable, TBaseTable } from "@/api/db/schema/base-entity";

export const users = createTable("users", {
    email: text("email").notNull(),
    name: text("name"),
});

export type TUser = TBaseTable<typeof users>;
export type TNewUser = TBaseInsert<typeof users>;
```

## CRUD Factory
- Use `useCrudFactory` from `@/shared/hooks/use-crud-factory` for all CRUD operations
- Factory requires operations object with: `getAll`, `create`, `update`, `delete`
- Operations must follow base entity patterns
- Example usage:
```typescript
function useUsers() {
    const operations = useMemo(() => ({
        getAll: async () => db.select().from(users),
        create: async (data: TNewUser) => db.insert(users).values(data).returning(),
        update: async (id: string, data: Partial<TNewUser>) => 
            db.update(users).set(data).where(eq(users.id, id)).returning(),
        delete: async (id: string) => 
            db.delete(users).where(eq(users.id, id))
    }), []);

    return useCrudFactory<TUser, TNewUser>(operations);
}
```

## Helpers
- use `@/core/shared/if-tauri` and `@/core/shared/if-web` to conditionally render components based on the platform. Some api's are only available in the tauri app, so you don't want to render components that use those api's on the web.

## Database

Don't ever touch the `drizzle.config.ts`
- The main schema is only responsible for re-exporting schemas comming from `src/module/MODULENAME/api/{MODULENAME-schema.ts,schemas/SUBMODULE-schemas.ts}`
- The `src/api/db/schema.ts` is only responsible for re-exporting the main schema
- The database can be imported like so: `import {db} from "@/api/db/connection.ts"`
- All database fields must use the appropriate helper functions:
  - `cuidId` for ID fields
  - `timestamps` for created_at/updated_at
  - These are available from `@/api/db/helpers`

## API layer
- Never use `route.ts` or api routes Only server functions.
- Inside each module there is an `api` folder. This folder contains the server functions.
- Each server function is namespaced by it's module. For example, the `tasks` module has a `tasks` folder inside the `api` folder. This folder contains all the server functions related to tasks. 
- Each api folder can consist of:
  - `mutations` - Server actions for creating/updating/deleting
  - `queries` - Server actions for fetching data
  - `MODULENAME-repository.ts` - Database operations
  - `schema` - Drizzle schemas using base entity
  - `validators` - Input validation
  - `services` - Complex business logic (when necessary)
- For CRUD operations we have a factory `useCrudFactory`
- All mutations/queries should return `TBaseResponse` or `TBaseMutationResponse`